Add a history_limit field to each job in jobs.yaml (integer, default 0). 0 means no history — keep current behavior exactly (live log only, nothing written to disk). Any value N > 0 means: persist up to the last N runs of that job to disk, deleting the oldest run once the count exceeds N.
Storage structure, for jobs with history_limit > 0:
logs/<job_id>/console_out/<run_number>/<job_id>_<run_number>_<timestamp>.log
Before each run, determine the next run_number by scanning existing numbered folders under that job’s console_out/ directory and taking the highest existing number + 1 (never reset to 0/1 on restart — this must keep incrementing indefinitely). After the run completes and its log is written, if the number of stored run folders for that job exceeds history_limit, delete the oldest (lowest-numbered) folder(s) until the count matches the limit again. Also write a small meta.json in each run folder with run number, start time, end time, and exit status, so history can be listed without re-parsing log files.
Continue streaming logs live over the existing WebSocket exactly as today — this disk write happens in parallel/alongside that, not instead of it.
New backend endpoints:
	•	GET /api/jobs/{job_id}/history — returns the list of retained runs for that job (run number, timestamp, status), most recent first. Return an empty list if history_limit is 0.
	•	GET /api/jobs/{job_id}/history/{run_number} — returns that run’s full stored log content, for static (non-live) display.
Frontend: add a small “History” chevron/arrow toggle to each job card — only rendered if that job’s history_limit is greater than 0. Expanding it shows a compact list of past runs (run number, timestamp, status badge), most recent first. Clicking a run opens the same log viewer/terminal component already used for live runs, loaded with that run’s static log content instead of a live stream. Keep the styling consistent with the existing card design — no new visual patterns.
